We can scan for networks that we can try to join.
```term
$ root@airside-ws01:~# nmcli device wifi list
IN-USE  BSSID              SSID                    MODE   CHAN  RATE       SIGNAL  BARS  SECURITY 
!!        02:00:00:00:00:00  HTB International WiFi  Infra  6     54 Mbit/s  100     ▂▄▆█  --       

IN-USE  BSSID              SSID                    MODE   CHAN  RATE       SIGNAL  BARS  SECURITY 
!!        02:00:00:00:00:00  HTB International WiFi  Infra  6     54 Mbit/s  100     ▂▄▆█  --      
```

We see the `HTB International WiFi` has the security mode set to `--`, that means there is no password needed to join the network. We can join the network and verify we get an IP.

```term
$ root@airside-ws01:~# nmcli device wifi connect "HTB International WiFi"
!!Device 'wlan2' successfully activated with '6040972f-4b47-4b75-b700-0ffb4b3dfa7f'.
??root@airside-ws01:~# ip addr show wlan2
6: wlan2: <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP> mtu 1500 qdisc noqueue state UP group default qlen 1000
    link/ether 02:00:00:00:02:00 brd ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff
!!    inet 10.13.37.182/24 brd 10.13.37.255 scope global dynamic noprefixroute wlan2
       valid_lft 43184sec preferred_lft 43184sec
    inet6 fe80::83cf:1ad9:d3b:e714/64 scope link noprefixroute 
       valid_lft forever preferred_lft forever
```

Since we are on wifi, we can create a virtual interface and use it to sniff the wifi traffic from the air. We can do so as such:
```term
??root@airside-ws01:~# iw phy phy2 interface add snf0 type monitor
??root@airside-ws01:~# ip link set wlan2 down
??root@airside-ws01:~# iw dev snf0 set channel 6
??root@airside-ws01:~# ip link set wlan2 up
??root@airside-ws01:~# ip link set snf0 up
```

We also have wireshark on the box, which makes for sniffing traffic quite easy. This could also be done with something like tcpdump.

Looking through the packets we get a hit on something valuable:

```term
